#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a6be11-8878-5070-81b7-b47676c4ce8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385292} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce9062b-d93f-55c2-a03a-1ae8c327c26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385293} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa04091-bc19-5979-8a5e-2e3897f35181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385294} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee0778f-6531-5ab8-bddf-60696be3a1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ee0778f-6531-5ab8-bddf-60696be3a1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ecdd4ec-27ca-5ae4-9688-c8b8aa4f79ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a08e9ea-1ab7-5988-bf4e-d55c1dd407e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343668" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=3$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 3 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e980a7-73e5-5db5-b117-dad8065a2ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508401} {:answer/id #uuid "7899c111-a756-5f84-bb7b-8a3a2fc1f66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508565} {:answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} {:answer/id #uuid "aff8259e-8a40-5b92-873a-8c1bbf4d08f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} :answer-field/id #uuid "820b3701-ddd6-58d9-9b7a-77aa5711efbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ccc73a6-d864-55e4-9da3-f78dd465c648" :question/math-academy-id "q-343671" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1db83d-b0ff-520c-a225-6d525cff002b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372856} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8ea9a4-c740-500c-9fab-6d37ceab5899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372857} {:answer/id #uuid "7e24b0bc-eb98-5df7-b3c2-9e5493a8cc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6e5693-da89-597a-8638-edeca9949654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e24b0bc-eb98-5df7-b3c2-9e5493a8cc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372858} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e4c22e-78c5-5e23-8e3c-f648b14c9d5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31df3052-c15b-5b42-8dc7-4a21ca73beb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344267" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $8$-periodic on $[-4,4]$, we have $L=4$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508605} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7031cc-3266-50a5-b23e-e80324be569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508652} {:answer/id #uuid "b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508660} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b8581d-d97f-5540-9faf-641daf95fba0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-344320" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1ee564-8e0f-5ee0-998f-2101fc9cd57a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372880} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1903c2-0865-5c88-ad0e-67617d3c44ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372881} {:answer/id #uuid "6005d9a8-46b9-5819-acc2-87a742a05929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372882} {:answer/id #uuid "3078353a-b59d-5067-b6f6-a1a830986836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3078353a-b59d-5067-b6f6-a1a830986836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372883} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f8df2d-ecbb-512a-aa1b-2ac650da1f39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b96aedd-ce80-5f05-9df5-d9873f821b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-344321" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=4$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 4 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}]}
